𝗝𝗲𝗱𝗶𝘀 is a popular Java library used to interact with Redis and I've used it for implementing our Rate Limiter because it provides a simple and intuitive API for executing Redis commands from JVM applications.

The first step is to store the count of requests for each sub-interval within the rolling window. Each request increments the counter for the current sub-interval.

Next, clean up of entries is performed that fall outside the rolling time window ensuring request counts for only active sub-intervals within the rolling window reside in Redis.

Finally, I retrieve all counts for the active sub-intervals summing them up to get the total number of requests within the rolling window. If it exceeds the limit, the new request is rejected else, allowed and the counter is updated.

